body:json {
  {
    "content": "title: React Core Challenge\ndescription: Are you ready to truly grasp the fundamental building blocks of React? The **React Core Challenge** is your essential interactive companion for mastering the foundational concepts that power modern web development. This isn't just a quiz; it's a dynamic, engaging experience meticulously designed to solidify your understanding of **React.js essentials.**\n\nDive deep into core topics including component structure, state and props management, lifecycle methods (or hooks for functional components), JSX syntax, and basic event handling. Each question is carefully crafted to test your knowledge comprehensively, providing instant, insightful feedback to reinforce correct answers and clarify any misconceptions.\n\nPerfect for aspiring React developers taking their first steps, or seasoned coders looking to refresh their memory on core principles before tackling complex projects or technical interviews. Identify your knowledge gaps, transform theoretical concepts into practical understanding, and build a rock-solid foundation.\n\nStop wondering, start mastering! Take the React Core Challenge now and confidently elevate your React expertise!\n\n---question---\ntype: mcq\nquestion: What is JSX in React?\noptions:\n1. A JavaScript linter for React code.\n2. A syntax extension for JavaScript that looks like HTML. [correct]\n3. A build tool used to compile React applications.\n4. A special type of React component.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: trueFalse\nquestion: React props are generally considered immutable within a child component.\ncorrect: true\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: written\nquestion: Explain the primary purpose of the `key` prop when rendering lists of elements in React.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: multiSelect\nquestion: Which of the following are fundamental concepts for defining and using a React component?\noptions:\n1. Returning JSX or null from its render/return function. [correct]\n2. Managing internal data using state. [correct]\n3. Receiving data from its parent using props. [correct]\n4. Directly manipulating the DOM using document.getElementById().\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: mcq\nquestion: What is the primary difference between `props` and `state` in React components?\noptions:\n1. props are mutable and state is immutable.\n2. props are used for internal component data, while state is for passing data to child components.\n3. props are passed from parent to child and are immutable, while state is managed within the component and can be changed. [correct]\n4. There is no significant difference; they are interchangeable.\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: trueFalse\nquestion: The `useState` hook can only be used in class components.\ncorrect: false\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: written\nquestion: Describe the main difference in how state and lifecycle methods are typically handled between class components and functional components (after React Hooks).\ntags: React\n\n---question---\ntype: multiSelect\nquestion: Which of the following are valid ways to attach an event handler to a DOM element within a React component's JSX?\noptions:\n1. Using a camelCase attribute like `onClick`. [correct]\n2. Assigning an arrow function directly to the event attribute. [correct]\n3. Using a string with JavaScript code, like `onclick=\"myFunction()\"`.\n4. Binding a class method to `this` and passing it. [correct]\ntags: React"
  }
}
